    {
      "text": "Resilient Distributed Dataset (RDD) is the fundamental data structure of Spark\n\nThere are times when higher-level manipulation will not meet the business or engineering problem you are trying to solve. For those cases, you\nmight need to use Spark’s lower-level APIs\n\n**There are two sets of low-level APIs**\n1. one for manipulating distributed data (RDDs)\n2. another for distributing and manipulating distributed shared variables (broadcast variables and\naccumulators).\n\n\n\n**When to Use the Low-Level APIs?**\n1. need very tight control over physical data placement across the cluster/partioner/skewness\n2. maintain some legacy codebase \n3. custom shared variable manupulation\n\n\n**What is RDD?**\n RDD represents an immutable, partitioned collection of records that can be operated\non in parallel. Unlike DataFrames though, where each record is a structured row containing\nfields with a known schema, in RDDs the records are just Java, Scala, or Python objects of the\nprogrammer’s choosing.\n",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/markdown",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "d079a53da7b34ff3a04a6"
    },
    {
      "text": "%spark\nwords.take(3)",
